From: MacGameVideoFix <https://github.com/MathiasKowoll/MacGameVideoFix>
Subject: [PATCH] winebus.sys: seize a DualSense on Bluetooth so macOS stops writing to it

WHERE THIS CAME FROM

MacGameVideoFix's own series, mgvf-NNNN, written on 2026-09-08, the fifth of
ours and the fourth in the controller-bus set. It is the first of that set to
touch the UNIX half of winebus rather than the PE half. Nothing in it is
specific to this project; it is offered to anyone carrying a patched winebus on
macOS.

THE FAULT

Two processes write to the pad, and the pad has room for one.

macOS drives a connected DualSense itself. WindowServer's
com.apple.GameController.HID:DualSense driver opens the pad ("Opening
HIDDevice") every time it appears and writes Bluetooth output reports to it, on
its own schedule, whether or not anything on the Mac is using the pad. winebus
opened the same pad SHARED -- IOHIDDeviceOpen(IOHIDDevice, 0) in
handle_DeviceMatchingCallback -- and wrote its own output reports through
IOHIDDeviceSetReport, which over Bluetooth is where the rumble, the lightbar
and the trigger effects go.

A DualSense on Bluetooth has one output pipe and it is not shared gracefully.
With wine writing to it too, macOS's writes time out.

HOW IT WAS MEASURED

From macOS's own log on 2026-09-08, not from inside wine:

  - 163 timeouts in one day, all of the same shape: "(Async) Unable to send BT
    output report to DualSense - error -536870186". That number is
    kIOReturnTimeout, 0xE00002D6.
  - Every one of them falls inside a minute in which a game was running under
    wine: 4 to 14 a minute in the afternoon sessions, and 20 to 35 a minute in
    the last one, where mgvf-0005's USB emulation had the guest writing to the
    pad far more often. Outside those minutes there are none.
  - After a burst, the driver tears itself down -- "Cancel", "Freeing
    HIDDevice" -- and 160 ms later bluetoothd drops the link: "ACL
    disconnected: <addr>, reason 10719". Six such drops in six minutes across
    the last two launches, against single, spread-out drops earlier in the day
    carrying reason 10722, which is the pad's ordinary idle power-off.

So the pad is not powering itself off during play. The link is being killed by
contention on the output pipe, and wine is the second writer.

THE CHANGE

Open the pad with kIOHIDOptionsTypeSeizeDevice instead of 0. IOKit then hands
the device to this process alone and macOS's own driver releases it, so there
is one writer on the pipe again.

The seize is not a preference; it is the mechanism, and it costs what it does
by design: while winebus holds the pad, macOS and its own applications cannot
use it. It is given back when the process that seized it goes away, which for
winebus is the bottle's winedevice.exe -- that is, when the bottle shuts down.

If the seizing open fails, the shared open this code always did is tried
instead, with a WARN. A device another process has already seized therefore
works exactly as it does today rather than disappearing, and no pad is ever
lost to this patch.

The close in handle_RemovalCallback is now given the options its open used. The
device lookup moves above the close to make that possible. Nothing measured
says IOKit needs it -- the open goes when the connection does -- but a close
that does not mirror its open is a question left for somebody else.

THE SCOPE

Seizing indiscriminately would be worse than the fault. winebus enumerates
whatever IOKit matches, and taking the user's keyboard or mouse away from macOS
while a bottle is up would be an unusable machine. Two things keep this narrow.

The enumeration already refuses everything that is not a joystick or a gamepad,
several lines above the open, and returns before opening it: the widest this
can ever reach is a game controller.

And the default is what was measured and nothing wider: a DualSense
(054c:0ce6, 054c:0df2) that arrived over BLUETOOTH. The timeouts were counted
on the Bluetooth output pipe; a pad on a cable has a different one and no
measurement of it, so a wired pad is still opened shared. A DualShock 4 is not
included either. The same shape is plausible for any pad macOS drives itself,
but plausible is not measured, and mgvf-0005 drew the line in the same place
for the same reason.

THE OPTION

  HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Services\winebus\Devices\<vid>/<pid>
    SeizeDevice  REG_DWORD  absent: the default above -- on for a DualSense on
                            Bluetooth, off for everything else. 0: open shared,
                            exactly as before this patch. Non-zero: seize.

ON by default for the pads it covers, unlike mgvf-0005's UsbEmulation, because
this repairs a defect rather than adding a behaviour: without it the link drops
mid-game. The value exists for the two people the default is wrong for -- a
DualSense owner who would rather share the pad with macOS while a bottle is up,
and the owner of some other pad who has measured the same timeouts and wants to
try it.

HOW THE OPTION REACHES THE UNIX SIDE

bus_iohid.c is the unix half and does not read the registry; it decides how to
open the device, so the answer has to be there. Rather than a second registry
reader in the unix half, or a new field in the unixlib interface, the value
rides the per-device settings that already cross the boundary: main.c's
load_device_options enumerates the Devices key at driver start into
options.devices, and struct bus_options -- that list included -- is handed to
each bus at init, which bus_iohid.c already keeps in its own "options". So
struct device_options gains one INT beside its existing "hidraw", read from the
same subkey by the same loop, -1 when the key says nothing so that the default
can differ per device. bus_iohid.c walks the list the way main.c's
is_hidraw_enabled does, with the same VID-only wildcard.

It is read once, at driver start, exactly like Hidraw: changing it takes effect
when the bottle's winedevice next starts.

NOT COVERED

A wired DualSense, a DualShock 4, and every other pad, by default -- see THE
SCOPE. The registry value reaches all of them for anyone who wants to measure.

Whether the seize repairs the drops has NOT been measured yet: what is measured
is the contention and its consequence, and that wine is the second writer. The
first run with this patch belongs in the same log the timeouts were counted in,
looking for the absence of "Unable to send BT output report".

What macOS does with a pad it has been asked to release mid-session is IOKit's
business and is not asserted here. What is asserted is only that the seizing
open, when it fails, falls back to the open this code always did.

---
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/bus_iohid.c
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/bus_iohid.c
@@ -113,6 +113,9 @@
     struct unix_device unix_device;
     IOHIDDeviceRef device;
     uint8_t *buffer;
+    /* MGVF mgvf-0006: the options the device was opened with, so that the
+     * close is the mirror of the open. */
+    IOOptionBits open_options;
 };
 
 static inline struct iohid_device *impl_from_unix_device(struct unix_device *iface)
@@ -266,7 +269,77 @@
     iohid_device_get_feature_report,
     iohid_device_set_feature_report,
 };
+
+/* MGVF mgvf-0006: whether to take the pad away from macOS while a bottle holds it.
+ *
+ * THE FAULT. macOS drives a connected DualSense itself: WindowServer's
+ * com.apple.GameController.HID:DualSense driver opens the pad every time it
+ * appears and writes Bluetooth output reports to it. winebus opened the same
+ * pad shared, below, and wrote its own output reports through
+ * IOHIDDeviceSetReport. Two writers on the pad's single Bluetooth output pipe
+ * is one too many. Measured from macOS's own log on 2026-09-08: macOS's writes
+ * time out -- "(Async) Unable to send BT output report to DualSense - error
+ * -536870186", which is kIOReturnTimeout -- 163 of them in a day, 4 to 35 a
+ * minute and only in the minutes a game was running under wine, the highest
+ * rate in the session where the guest wrote most often. After a burst its
+ * driver tears itself down ("Cancel", "Freeing HIDDevice") and 160 ms later
+ * bluetoothd drops the link: "ACL disconnected ... reason 10719", six times in
+ * six minutes across two launches, where the drops earlier in the day carried
+ * 10722, the pad's ordinary idle power-off. The pad is not turning itself off
+ * during play; the link is being killed by contention on the output pipe, and
+ * wine is the second writer.
+ *
+ * THE CHANGE. Open with kIOHIDOptionsTypeSeizeDevice, which hands the device
+ * to this process alone and makes macOS's driver release it, so there is only
+ * one writer again. The cost is the mechanism, not a side effect: while a
+ * bottle holds the pad, macOS and its own applications cannot use it. It is
+ * given back when the process that seized it goes away, which for winebus is
+ * the bottle's winedevice.exe.
+ *
+ * THE SCOPE. Seizing indiscriminately would take the user's own input devices
+ * away from macOS, so the default is what the measurement covers and nothing
+ * wider: a DualSense (054c:0ce6, 054c:0df2) that arrived over Bluetooth, which
+ * is where the timeouts were counted. A DualShock 4 is not included -- the same
+ * shape is plausible for any pad macOS drives, but plausible is not measured,
+ * and mgvf-0005 drew the line in the same place. Nothing else is seized unless
+ * a registry value says so:
+ *
+ *   HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Services\winebus\Devices\<vid>/<pid>
+ *     SeizeDevice  REG_DWORD  absent: the default above. 0: open shared, as
+ *                             before. Non-zero: seize.
+ *
+ * Unlike mgvf-0005's UsbEmulation this one is ON by default for the pads it
+ * covers, because it repairs a defect rather than adding a behaviour: without
+ * it the link drops mid-game. The value is there for the two people the
+ * default is wrong for -- a DualSense owner who would rather share the pad
+ * with macOS, and the owner of some other pad who has measured the same
+ * timeouts and wants to try it.
+ *
+ * The value cannot reach a keyboard or a mouse: the enumeration below refuses
+ * everything that is not a joystick or a gamepad before it ever opens
+ * anything, so the widest this can be turned on for is a game controller.
+ */
+static BOOL want_seize(const struct device_desc *desc)
+{
+    const struct device_options *device;
+    BOOL seize;
 
+    seize = desc->bus_type == BUS_TYPE_BLUETOOTH && is_dualsense_gamepad(desc->vid, desc->pid);
+
+    /* The per-device options the PE side read at driver start, handed to this
+     * bus in bus_options at init. An entry with no SeizeDevice value leaves
+     * the default alone; an entry with one decides it either way. */
+    if (options) LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY(device, &options->devices, struct device_options, entry)
+    {
+        if (device->vid != desc->vid) continue;
+        if (device->pid != -1 && device->pid != desc->pid) continue;
+        if (device->seize == -1) continue;
+        return !!device->seize;
+    }
+
+    return seize;
+}
+
 static void handle_DeviceMatchingCallback(void *context, IOReturn result, void *sender, IOHIDDeviceRef IOHIDDevice)
 {
     struct device_desc desc =
@@ -275,6 +348,7 @@
         .serialnumber = {'0','0','0','0',0},
     };
     struct iohid_device *impl;
+    IOOptionBits open_options;
     USAGE_AND_PAGE usages;
     CFStringRef str;
 
@@ -306,8 +380,22 @@
         return;
     }
 
-    if (IOHIDDeviceOpen(IOHIDDevice, 0) != kIOReturnSuccess)
+    /* MGVF mgvf-0006: seize where it is wanted, and fall back to the shared
+     * open this always did when the seize is refused -- another process may
+     * already hold the device exclusively, and a pad shared with macOS behaves
+     * exactly as it did before this patch. */
+    open_options = want_seize(&desc) ? kIOHIDOptionsTypeSeizeDevice : 0;
+    if (open_options && IOHIDDeviceOpen(IOHIDDevice, open_options) != kIOReturnSuccess)
     {
+        WARN("Failed to seize HID device %p (vid %04x, pid %04x); opening it shared, as before\n",
+             IOHIDDevice, desc.vid, desc.pid);
+        open_options = 0;
+    }
+    else if (open_options)
+        TRACE("Seized HID device %p (vid %04x, pid %04x): macOS cannot use it while this process holds it\n",
+              IOHIDDevice, desc.vid, desc.pid);
+    if (!open_options && IOHIDDeviceOpen(IOHIDDevice, 0) != kIOReturnSuccess)
+    {
         ERR("Failed to open HID device %p (vid %04x, pid %04x)\n", IOHIDDevice, desc.vid, desc.pid);
         return;
     }
@@ -333,6 +421,7 @@
     list_add_tail(&device_list, &impl->unix_device.entry);
     impl->device = IOHIDDevice;
     impl->buffer = NULL;
+    impl->open_options = open_options;
 
     bus_event_queue_device_created(&event_queue, &impl->unix_device, &desc);
 }
@@ -346,9 +435,14 @@
     /* Note: Yes, we leak the buffer. But according to research there is no
              safe way to deallocate that buffer. */
     IOHIDDeviceUnscheduleFromRunLoop(IOHIDDevice, CFRunLoopGetCurrent(), kCFRunLoopDefaultMode);
-    IOHIDDeviceClose(IOHIDDevice, 0);
 
+    /* MGVF mgvf-0006: looked up before the close, so the close can be given the
+     * options the open used. Nothing measured says IOKit needs them -- the open
+     * is released when the connection goes -- but a close that does not mirror
+     * its open is a question nobody should have to answer later. */
     impl = find_device_from_iohid(IOHIDDevice);
+    IOHIDDeviceClose(IOHIDDevice, impl ? impl->open_options : 0);
+
     if (impl) bus_event_queue_device_removed(&event_queue, &impl->unix_device);
     else WARN("failed to find device for iohid device %p\n", IOHIDDevice);
 }
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/main.c
@@ -1291,6 +1291,7 @@
     device->vid = vid;
     device->pid = pid;
     device->hidraw = -1;
+    device->seize = -1;
 
     LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY(next, &options.devices, struct device_options, entry)
         if (next->vid > vid || (next->vid == vid && next->pid > pid)) break;
@@ -1322,6 +1323,8 @@
     while (!status && name)
     {
         static const UNICODE_STRING hidraw = RTL_CONSTANT_STRING(L"Hidraw");
+        /* MGVF mgvf-0006 */
+        static const UNICODE_STRING seize = RTL_CONSTANT_STRING(L"SeizeDevice");
         static const UNICODE_STRING backslash = RTL_CONSTANT_STRING(L"\\");
         struct device_options *device;
         UNICODE_STRING name_str;
@@ -1358,6 +1361,16 @@
             device->hidraw = *(DWORD *)info->Data;
         if (device->hidraw != -1) TRACE("- %04x/%04x: %sabling hidraw\n", device->vid, device->pid, device->hidraw ? "en" : "dis");
 
+        /* MGVF mgvf-0006: read here, beside Hidraw, because the answer is
+         * wanted on the UNIX side -- bus_iohid.c decides how to open the
+         * device -- and this list is the only per-device setting that already
+         * crosses that boundary, in the bus_options handed to each bus at
+         * init. Read once at driver start, like Hidraw: changing it takes
+         * effect when the bottle's winedevice next starts. */
+        if (!NtQueryValueKey(subkey, &seize, KeyValuePartialInformation, info, sizeof(buffer), &size) && info->Type == REG_DWORD)
+            device->seize = *(DWORD *)info->Data;
+        if (device->seize != -1) TRACE("- %04x/%04x: %sseizing the device from the host\n", device->vid, device->pid, device->seize ? "" : "not ");
+
         NtClose(subkey);
     }
 
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/unixlib.h
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/unixlib.h
@@ -53,6 +53,9 @@
     UINT vid;
     UINT pid;
     INT hidraw;
+    /* MGVF mgvf-0006: -1 when the device's key says nothing, so that the
+     * default can differ per device rather than being a single global one. */
+    INT seize;
 };
 
 struct bus_options
